 ],
 "literal": "The bulls indeed are young, they do not understand the yoke / The cart is heavily loaded with goods / Whether descending into a low valley or climbing up a high ground / 'Who knows what will happen there?' thinking thus, the salt-merchants / Keep a spare axle tied to the under-wood, just like that / O great one with a famous, protecting hand! You are like the moon / On the day of the full moon. Darkness / Where indeed is it for those who live in your shade?",
 "translation": "The bulls are young and do not yet know the yoke, but the cart is heavily laden with goods. Whether descending into a low valley or climbing up a steep hill, the salt-merchants, wondering who can foresee what might happen there, keep a spare axle tied to the chassis. Like that spare axle, you are a source of safety, O great one of illustrious generosity! You are like the full moon on its perfect day. How can darkness ever reach those who live under your protective shade?",
 "modern": "எருதுகள் இளையவை, அவை நுகத்தடியை அறியாதவை. வண்டியில் பண்டங்கள் மிக அதிகமாக ஏற்றப்பட்டுள்ளன. பள்ளத்தில் இறங்கும்போதும் மேட்டில் ஏறும்போதும் அங்கே என்ன நடக்கும் என்று அறியக்கூடியவர் யார் என்று அஞ்சி, உப்பு வணிகர்கள் வண்டியின் கீழ்மரத்தில் ஒரு மாற்று அச்சை (சேம அச்சு)க் கட்டி வைப்பார்கள். அதுபோல, புகழ் விளங்கும் கொடையை உடைய நெடியோனே! நீ நாள் நிறைந்த முழு நிலவைப் போன்றவன். உன்னுடைய நிழலில் வாழ்பவர்களுக்கு இருள் என்பது எங்கே இருக்கப் போகிறது?",
 "explanation": "In this Pāṭāṇ poem, the poet Avvaiyār praises Pokuṭṭeḻiṉi, the son of her patron Atiyamāṉ. She uses a vivid simile of salt-merchants traveling with a heavily laden cart pulled by young, inexperienced bulls. Fearing the rough terrain of valleys and hills, the merchants wisely tie a spare axle (sēma-accu) beneath the cart for safety. The poet compares Pokuṭṭeḻiṉi to this spare axle, highlighting his role as a reliable protector and support for his father's kingdom. Finally, she compares him to the full moon, whose brilliant light completely dispels the darkness of distress for all those who live under his protective shade.",
